<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/rock-cycle">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Rocks are not forever — they are <em>temporary forms</em> in a planetary recycling loop. The
    stone of a future mountain is, right now, probably seafloor mud; the pebbles in your garden
    path were once the inside of a volcano. Geology's great insight, won slowly against religious
    and scientific resistance alike, is <strong>deep time</strong>: James Hutton, standing at
    Siccar Point on the Scottish coast in 1788, read a vanished mountain range buried inside
    seafloor layers tilted and eroded before more layers were laid on top — and realised the
    Earth's history needed not thousands of years but an almost inconceivable span, with "no
    vestige of a beginning, no prospect of an end". The same material can be igneous,
    sedimentary and metamorphic, over and over, across billions of years.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three great classes mark the three states of the loop: <strong>igneous</strong> rock is
    frozen magma; <strong>sedimentary</strong> rock is assembled debris; <strong>metamorphic</strong>
    rock is older rock transformed, solid, by heat and pressure. Scrub through one full
    revolution below — at mountain-belt pace, a few tens of millions of years per lap.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the loop as steps — scrub it like a cell dividing, but at mountain-belt pace" widget="timeline" config={rockTimeline} />

  <h2>The loop, node by node</h2>
  <p>
    The cycle is easier to navigate as a map, because there is more than one route through it.
    Click the stops below: any rock can be melted back to magma, weathered to sediment, or
    metamorphosed in place — and each of those outcomes can itself be revisited. A granite can
    erode into sand, become sandstone, then quartzite, then melt; a shale can skip the sediment
    stage entirely and transform straight to schist under a mountain range.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the recycling map — click any rock type to see how it leaves, and how it returns" widget="diagram" config={cycleCfg} />

  <h2>Two engines drive the wheel</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing about the rock cycle happens for free, and its power comes from two mismatched
    engines. Earth's <strong>internal heat</strong> — left over from the planet's formation and
    topped up continuously by radioactive decay — drives the deep half: melting, volcanism,
    subduction and mountain-building, all organised by plate tectonics, which acts as the
    cycle's conveyor belt (subduction drags surface rock down toward the melting zone; collisions
    uplift it back). Sunlight, meanwhile, powers the shallow half: weather evaporates water,
    rain freezes and thaws, rivers run, and all of that weathers rock into grains and carries
    them to the sea. The rock cycle is therefore half geology, half solar power — a rare machine
    driven simultaneously from below and above.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two engines also set the pace. Surface processes (weathering, erosion, deposition) are
    comparatively quick: a river can carve a valley in a million years, and sediments buried in
    a basin can harden to rock within a few million. Deep processes are slow: subduction
    delivers rock to the melting zone over tens of millions of years, and the fastest regional
    metamorphism still takes millions. The cycle turns no faster than its slowest arc — which
    is why, despite four and a half billion years of operation, the loop is nowhere near
    "finished".
  </p>

  <h2>The three families in the field</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Igneous rocks</strong> are hard, crystalline and unlayered, and their texture tells
    you their cooling story. Cool slowly deep underground (as granite does) and crystals have
    time to grow large enough to see; erupt as lava (basalt) and the same chemistry freezes so
    fast that crystals stay microscopic; quench instantly and you get obsidian, a natural glass.
    Giant's Causeway in Northern Ireland is basalt that shrank into hexagonal columns as it
    cooled — geometry from physics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Sedimentary rocks</strong> are layered, usually softer, and often fossil-bearing —
    the archive of Earth's surface history. Sandstone was once sand (a river bed, a beach, a
    desert dune); shale was compacted mud on a quiet seafloor; limestone is built of shells and
    reef debris. The white cliffs of Dover are chalk made of uncountable marine algae plates.
    Because sediments pile up from the bottom, their layers record time and environment, which
    is why sedimentary rocks carry most of the fossil record and most of the story chapters in
    the geologic-time lesson.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Metamorphic rocks</strong> are the banded, streaked, sometimes sparkly ones — slate
    roofs, marble statues, gneiss shields. Their appearance is a pressure record: the more
    tortured the banding, the harder the rock was squeezed. Two field clues separate them from
    layered sedimentary rocks: metamorphic layers (foliation) are <em>mineral alignments</em>,
    wavy and often folded, while sedimentary layers are grain deposits, flat and clean; and
    metamorphic rocks resist — they are what rock becomes after surviving conditions that would
    have destroyed it once already.
  </p>

  <h2>Metamorphism: cooking without melting</h2>
  <p>
    Metamorphism changes rock <em>in the solid state</em>. Bury a shale — heat climbs with depth
    at roughly 25–30 °C per kilometre, and pressure grows with the weight above — and at a few
    kilometres down the clay minerals become unstable and rebuild into micas, all while the rock
    stays solid. Push further and new, denser minerals form in sequence. This is why
    metamorphism sits between sedimentation and melting: hot enough to rearrange atoms, not hot
    enough to melt. <em>Common misconception:</em> metamorphic rock is not "partly melted rock".
    If the rock melts, it becomes magma and leaves the metamorphic club entirely — the
    metamorphic world is strictly solid-state chemistry, which is what makes its textures so
    distinctive.
  </p>
  <p>
    The three agents act differently. <strong>Heat</strong> drives new mineral growth;
    <strong>pressure</strong> (equal in all directions — confining pressure) compresses pores and
    recrystallises; <strong>differential stress</strong> — squeezing harder in one direction —
    aligns minerals, creating the flat fabrics below; and <strong>chemically active fluids</strong>
    ferry atoms around like solvents, speeding every reaction. Contact metamorphism (an intrusion
    baking its wall rock — marble around a granite) is heat-dominated and fabric-free; regional
    metamorphism (whole mountain belts) is the full squeeze. The classic upgrades: shale → slate
    → schist → gneiss as intensity rises; limestone → marble; sandstone → quartzite.
  </p>

  <h2>Foliation and lineation: the pressure record</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Foliation</strong> is any planar fabric imposed by differential stress. Its
    mechanism is simple and beautiful: platy minerals like mica, growing under a directional
    squeeze, orient themselves perpendicular to the maximum compression — like cards fanned
    flat when you press a deck between your palms. At low grades the alignment is a fine
    <em>cleavage</em> (slate splits into clean roofing sheets along it); at higher grades the
    micas grow larger and the rock becomes glittery <em>schist</em>; at the highest grades
    light and dark minerals segregate into stripes — <em>gneissic banding</em>. <strong>Lineation</strong>
    is the companion fabric: elongate minerals or stretched pebbles aligned like grain in wood,
    recording the direction of flow. A geologist can therefore read not just how hot and
    squeezed a rock was, but <em>which way</em> the squeeze came from — foliation is a
    fossilised compass.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not all metamorphic rocks are foliated. Marble (from limestone) and quartzite (from
    sandstone) are <em>non-foliated</em>, because their parent minerals — calcite, quartz —
    grow into interlocking equant grains rather than plates: there is nothing to align. Foliation
    needs platy or needle-like minerals, which is why mudrocks make the most dramatic slates
    and why a marble quarry wall shows no layering at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Metamorphic grade and facies</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Grade</strong> is the intensity of metamorphism, and it is read from
    <em>index minerals</em> that are stable only in certain temperature windows. Barrovian
    zones (after George Barrow, mapping Scotland in the 1890s) march from chlorite through
    biotite, garnet and staurolite to kyanite and sillimanite: find garnet, you are in moderate
    grade; find sillimanite, the rock has been near its melting ceiling. In slate the original
    clay is barely transformed; in gneiss the rock is on magma's doorstep.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Metamorphic facies</strong> generalise the idea to pressure as well as temperature:
    each facies is a P–T neighbourhood with its own mineral suite. Greenschist and amphibolite
    mark ordinary burial and mountain-belt conditions; granulite marks the hot lower crust; and
    <em>blueschist</em> — cold but crushing pressure — is the fingerprint of subduction zones,
    where cold slabs dive fast enough to be squeezed without being warmed. Reading a facies out
    of a rock is reading the tectonic setting it passed through: the rock cycle's map, and the
    plate tectonics lesson's story, compressed into mineral choices.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the rock cycle is not a neat circle with one track. Rocks
  skip stages constantly — igneous can metamorphose directly, sedimentary can melt directly,
  and some rocks cycle many times without ever visiting another class. The diagram above is a
  road network, not a ferris wheel.</p>

  <h2>Identifying a rock: the field routine</h2>
  <p>
    Geologists classify a hand sample with a routine you can run on any pebble. First ask
    <em>are there layers, and are they mineral alignments or deposited grains?</em> —
    separating metamorphic from sedimentary. Then check <em>grain size and texture</em>:
    interlocking crystals with no layering says igneous, and the crystal size says how fast
    it cooled (coarse = intrusive granite, fine = extruded basalt, glassy = quenched
    obsidian). Fossils or rounded grains cemented together say sedimentary; drop dilute acid
    on it and a fizz pins limestone (calcite) instantly; a light, soapy-feeling grey-white
    block that scratches glass is quartzite (metamorphosed sandstone); a rock that splits
    into flat roofing sheets along a silky sheen is slate. A three-question field test —
    <em>layered? crystals? fossils?</em> — plus one acid bottle sorts the entire rock
    kingdom into its first box, and every museum drawer in the world is organised by some
    version of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Deep time has a paper trail. Hutton's Siccar Point "unconformity" — tilted red sandstone
    beneath horizontal grey one, with the missing eroded interval spanning unknown millions of
    years — became the founding argument for uniformitarianism: the idea that today's processes
    explain the past ("the present is the key to the past"). The oldest surviving crustal rocks
    are metamorphic survivors: the Acasta gneiss of northwest Canada at about 4.03 billion
    years, with older (though disputed) claims from the Nuvvuagittuq belt; and the single
    oldest known Earth material is a zircon crystal from the Jack Hills of Australia dated to
    about 4.4 billion years — a grain that outlived every rock around it because zircons are
    almost indestructible and carry uranium, nature's own clock, inside them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Modern metamorphic petrology is quantitative: from the minerals present and their
    compositions, thermodynamic models reconstruct the exact pressure–temperature–time path a
    rock followed — up to 800 °C and 15,000 atmospheres and back. The paths reveal deep-earth
    mechanics: rocks buried at 60–80 km in subduction zones (eclogite facies) that resurfaced
    intact, a process called exhumation that geologists are still arguing over; metamorphic
    "core complexes" where the deep crust rises like a raisin in rising dough; and fluids
    released from descending plates that trigger melting above them — the reason the Pacific's
    "Ring of Fire" exists. Every one of those stories was read out of solid rock, using the
    fabrics and facies this chapter introduced: geology's version of forensics, where the
    evidence has been sitting under a mountain range for fifty million years.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hutton, <em>Theory of the Earth</em> (1788/1795); Barrow, "On an Intrusive Gneiss" (1893); USGS, "The Rock Cycle"; OpenStax, <em>An Introduction to Geology</em>, chapters 6–8; Spear, <em>Metamorphic Phase Equilibria and Pressure-Temperature-Time Paths</em>.</p>
